1
00:00:00,470 --> 00:00:03,170
Someone made a strange mistake while designing this

2
00:00:03,170 --> 00:00:06,130
giant ship. At least, that's what it looks

3
00:00:06,130 --> 00:00:08,510
like. There's a massive bump sticking out from

4
00:00:08,510 --> 00:00:09,850
underneath its nose.

5
00:00:09,850 --> 00:00:11,830
And if you're trying to build a ship

6
00:00:11,830 --> 00:00:15,230
that moves efficiently through water, adding an awkward-looking

7
00:00:15,230 --> 00:00:18,110
bulge seems like the last thing you'd want

8
00:00:18,110 --> 00:00:19,850
to do. But here's the twist.

9
00:00:19,850 --> 00:00:21,950
That strange bump isn't a mistake.

10
00:00:21,950 --> 00:00:24,290
It's actually designed to help the ship move

11
00:00:24,290 --> 00:00:26,290
faster while using less fuel.

12
00:00:26,290 --> 00:00:28,390
And the secret is surprisingly clever.

13
00:00:28,994 --> 00:00:31,434
You see, when a giant cargo ship moves

14
00:00:31,434 --> 00:00:34,414
through the ocean, it doesn't simply push water

15
00:00:34,414 --> 00:00:35,334
out of its way.

16
00:00:35,334 --> 00:00:36,654
It creates waves.

17
00:00:36,654 --> 00:00:39,494
Look at the front of a moving ship.

18
00:00:39,494 --> 00:00:42,454
Water gets pushed aside, rises, and forms a

19
00:00:42,454 --> 00:00:44,254
large wave around the bow.

20
00:00:44,254 --> 00:00:46,094
This is called the bow wave.

21
00:00:46,094 --> 00:00:48,314
And creating it requires energy.

22
00:00:48,314 --> 00:00:51,614
The ship's engines must constantly work against the

23
00:00:51,614 --> 00:00:54,394
resistance created as it moves through the water.

24
00:00:54,394 --> 00:00:56,374
Now, engineers had a problem.

25
00:00:56,374 --> 00:00:59,834
They could install more powerful engines, but that

26
00:00:59,834 --> 00:01:03,214
would mean burning more fuel and increasing operating costs.

27
00:01:03,865 --> 00:01:05,925
So they came up with a much stranger

28
00:01:05,925 --> 00:01:09,085
solution. Instead of making the engine stronger…

29
00:01:09,085 --> 00:01:11,005
They changed the ship's nose.

30
00:01:11,005 --> 00:01:12,765
They added a second nose.

31
00:01:12,765 --> 00:01:14,545
Not above the water.

32
00:01:14,545 --> 00:01:16,645
Below it. It's called a bulbous bow.

33
00:01:16,645 --> 00:01:19,585
It's that rounded extension sticking out from underneath

34
00:01:19,585 --> 00:01:21,525
the front of many large ships.

35
00:01:21,525 --> 00:01:23,105
And it isn't decorative.

36
00:01:23,105 --> 00:01:24,625
It has a very specific job.

37
00:01:25,186 --> 00:01:27,786
As the ship moves forward, the underwater bulb

38
00:01:27,786 --> 00:01:30,126
pushes water around it and creates its own

39
00:01:30,126 --> 00:01:33,386
wave system. Now, here's where things get interesting.

40
00:01:33,386 --> 00:01:35,866
The bulb is carefully designed so that the

41
00:01:35,866 --> 00:01:38,446
waves it produces interact with the waves created

42
00:01:38,446 --> 00:01:40,226
by the main body of the ship.

43
00:01:40,226 --> 00:01:43,246
Under the right conditions, these waves can partially

44
00:01:43,246 --> 00:01:44,426
cancel each other out.

45
00:01:44,994 --> 00:01:47,414
Think about throwing two stones into a pond.

46
00:01:47,414 --> 00:01:49,114
Each stone creates ripples.

47
00:01:49,114 --> 00:01:52,374
When those ripples meet, some places become more

48
00:01:52,374 --> 00:01:55,394
turbulent, while other places become surprisingly calm.

49
00:01:55,394 --> 00:01:57,734
Something similar happens around a ship.

50
00:01:57,734 --> 00:02:00,194
The bulb creates a wave that can weaken

51
00:02:00,194 --> 00:02:01,994
part of the ship's main bow wave.

52
00:02:01,994 --> 00:02:05,374
And when wave-making resistance is reduced, the ship

53
00:02:05,374 --> 00:02:07,614
doesn't need to work as hard to maintain

54
00:02:07,614 --> 00:02:10,194
its speed. That can mean less engine power

55
00:02:10,194 --> 00:02:11,834
and lower fuel consumption.

56
00:02:12,313 --> 00:02:14,513
But if this design is so clever, why

57
00:02:14,513 --> 00:02:16,413
doesn't every ship have one?

58
00:02:16,413 --> 00:02:18,153
Well, there's a catch.

59
00:02:18,153 --> 00:02:20,413
A bulbous bow doesn't work equally well at

60
00:02:20,413 --> 00:02:22,793
every speed. Its shape and size must be

61
00:02:22,793 --> 00:02:25,353
carefully matched to the ship's design and normal

62
00:02:25,353 --> 00:02:28,273
operating conditions. At the right speed, it can

63
00:02:28,273 --> 00:02:31,233
reduce resistance. But at slower speeds or under

64
00:02:31,233 --> 00:02:34,753
different loading conditions, that extra underwater surface can

65
00:02:34,753 --> 00:02:36,213
actually increase resistance.

66
00:02:36,704 --> 00:02:39,724
That's why naval engineers test different designs using

67
00:02:39,724 --> 00:02:42,884
computer simulations and miniature ship models in special

68
00:02:42,884 --> 00:02:45,564
water tanks. They study how water flows around

69
00:02:45,564 --> 00:02:48,464
the hull, how waves interact, and how much

70
00:02:48,464 --> 00:02:50,404
power the ship needs to move.

71
00:02:50,404 --> 00:02:53,104
Because these ships aren't making just one journey.

72
00:02:53,104 --> 00:02:56,884
They can spend years travelling between continents, carrying

73
00:02:56,884 --> 00:02:59,704
everything from electronics and clothing to food and

74
00:02:59,704 --> 00:03:03,164
machinery. Even a relatively small improvement in efficiency

75
00:03:03,164 --> 00:03:06,224
can become significant over thousands of operating hours.

76
00:03:06,757 --> 00:03:08,797
So the next time you see a gigantic

77
00:03:08,797 --> 00:03:11,677
cargo ship crossing the ocean, take another look

78
00:03:11,677 --> 00:03:12,757
at its nose.

79
00:03:12,757 --> 00:03:15,877
That strange underwater bump isn't a design mistake.

80
00:03:15,877 --> 00:03:18,097
It's a carefully engineered wave-maker.

81
00:03:18,097 --> 00:03:20,437
A design that helps a massive vessel fight

82
00:03:20,437 --> 00:03:22,357
the resistance of the ocean…

83
00:03:22,357 --> 00:03:24,077
By creating a wave of its own.
